aceable to the diabetes  control and complications trial  dcct reference values  again the ngsp website lists all  certified platforms  more practical considerations will  include  assay susceptibility to interferences  and the ability of the test platform  to detect or avoid interferences  additional considerations include sample  throughput maintenance  requirements and certainly cost to the  pair in the laboratory  additional general considerations  include an assessment of  locally available resources and  established  laboratory workflows adding a1c to  existing instrumentation that has  additional test capacity and redundancy  has many advantages may even allow for  automation and integration into an  already  streamlined process as with  any new test offering ip resources  capabilities and functionality must also  be considered  lastly recognizing that any a1c method  may have interferences or limitations  i believe it's very important to  understand what tests are available from  your  reference laboratory and how they might  be of assistance  the goal of the laboratory in the  pre-analytic phase of a1c testing should  be to enable the provider to select the  most appropriate test for the individual  patient  it is critical to recognize that there  are biological factors that can  interfere with the actual measurement of  a1c  and there are other factors that can  influence the interpretation  of the a1c result these biological  factors  may be method dependent or method  independent  the status of the patient's health and  past medical history  can help navigate acid dependent  limitations  for instance a provider may already know  that a patient  has a particular hemoglobin variant  especially if this patient is being  thoroughly monitored for glycemic  control  the provider may also know that this  variant interferes with their  in-house methods that are readily  available to them  in this situation it would be most  prudent for the provider to have the  ability to order an alternative method  at order entry to avoid the risk of  erroneous test results as well as to  minimize  extra cost and prolonged turnaround time  the provider may also already know that  a patient has a variant that does not  interfere with their in-house methods  however even in these situations the  provider  may or may not appreciate that the  variant that is not  analytically interfering may still  influence the a1c result because of the  variant's influence on  red cell survival and how that will  impact the final  a1c result  because a1c results are dependent on red  cell survival  to accurate reflect average blood  glucose concentrations  any changes in the red cell lifespan may  result in an a1c  measurement that fails to accurately  reflect average glycemia during the  previous  120 days or so other factors that affect  glycation  and are independent of the method used  include recent transfusions anemias  polycythemia  pregnancy renal impairment as well as  individuals with homozygous or compound  heterozygous variants  so the priority in the pre-analytic  phase  should be to enable the provider with  appropriate test options order entry  ideally  it should be apparent at order entry  whether options are for  monitoring versus diagnosis what the  testing platform  is that will be utilized for the test  that they chose  and the limitations of those methods  should be outlined for that physician as  well as alternative  tests available to order  each a1c method has unique challenges to  consider in the analytic  phase of testing and it's important to  consider  that some a1c method vendors actually  have multiple  assays that are different and they're  commercially available  and each will have its own advantages  and challenges  there are many resources however  available in the literature and online  and these resources do a great job at  describing assay specific interferences  the ability of the system to recognize  when an interference is present  and if that system is actually capable  of withholding results  if an interference is detected some  systems can also detect an interference  while still providing an accurate aoc  result  the vast majority of errors related to  reporting and accurate  results can often be avoided by simply  integrating  the instructions and guidance that many  manufacturers provide  and integrating those into the  laboratory standard operating procedures  laboratories should also consider  providing resources and tools  to their staff to enable them to know  how to address specific interferences  tools such as  job aids reference charts prior results  and  validation data can certainly help  in the reference lab setting it's not  uncommon for us to receive samples that  were first tested at the primary site  and found to have an interference by  that their method  so it can be very helpful for labs to  know what instrumentation a reference  laboratory is used  and when those methods should be used to  avoid reporting out incorrect values  when an interference is present  now the post analytic phase of testing  is another opportunity for  us to communicate back to the provider  and provide guidance as needed  a number of elements can and probably  should be included in the full result  report  however it's really important for us to  keep in mind that the lab  results in many electronic health  records only display the final result of  the test  and notes or comments are often only  seen if a provider clicks into that  result to investigate  so it's prudent to only display a result  for a1c  when there's complete confidence in the  results if there's any concern about the  accuracy of the result  a placeholder in the result field such  as a comment like  the node will direct the provider to the  comment section of the report  the report can or should include the  following elements as well  the test method used information about  detected variants  published metrics for diabetes for the  diagnosis or  even glycemic targets which may  or may not be institutionally derived or  maybe from  published guidelines and lastly the  report should be  clear to both the provider and the  patient  if a method interference is observed and  cannot be resolved  the lab should make recommendations for  appropriate follow-up testing  or have this set up as a potentially as  a reflexive test  also the report can direct providers to  know what alternative methods are  available to monitor  monitor glycemic control such as  fructosamine glycated albumin or  anhydroglucitol  so in situations where a lab method  cannot directly detect hemoglobin  variants  the lab can still provide some guidance  to physicians on detecting  potentially erroneous a1c results  this guidance may be included in the  result comments on the report or  on an informational lab page  for example some reasons to be  suspicious of an a1c result include  observing that a self-monitored glucose  does not correlate with the a1c result  an a1c value greater than 15 or  less than 4 percent or a significant  change in  a1c results by two different methods  um there is additional guidance on the  niddq website  for patients and providers the only  problem with this approach is that  it's really trying to detect a false  result instead of  avoiding it and largely their  responsibility  rests with the physician to be able to  detect these issues  so to finish up my presentation i just  want to talk about a few more  suggestions in the literature  as to how labs might consider setting up  workflows for a1c testing to avoid  erroneous reporting we will quickly  cover a few of those ideas here  one idea is that for patients who are at  a higher risk  for variance hemoglobin electrophoresis  could be performed first as a screen  if the result is normal essentially any  a1c  test would suffice the lab could also  choose to employ  a hemoglobin or excusing a method that  is capable of identifying common  variants  or having algorithmic or reflexive  testing to have the specimen analyzed by  other methods  when the result is suspected to be  incorrect  to summarize uh this topic of choosing  an a1c method for your laboratory  it is certainly important to keep in  mind and to consider  as you would a risk assessment the  pre-analytic analytic and post-analytic  phases of the testing  process we should also always keep in  mind the impacts to the patients  the providers and also to our  laboratorians  choose only ngsp approved methods  and provide a comprehensive solution to  ensure the most accurate  and appropriate results are provided  thank you for joining me today  thank you randy and kelly for your  informative presentations  we'll now start the live q a if you have  a question  click on the answer question ask a  question box  located in the far left of your screen  and we'll try to answer as many of your  questions  as time permits so let's get started  the first question  [Music]  is can you comment on the impact  of chemically modified hemoglobin  acetylated and cobamulated on different  methods  thanks kelly if you wouldn't mind  addressing that question  yeah certainly so not every method will  be  affected by either acetylated or  carbenylated  hemoglobin a1c but i  would certainly point anyone who is  considering a different hemoglobin a1c  method  to the ngsp website where dr little had  demonstrated that there are uh resources  and tables available  that actually reference literature and  references in the literature to  help guide and provide guidance as to  which specific assays have  interferences  thank you next question  is what would be the hemoglobin a1c high  cut off value  where the medical treatment would not  change no matter how high the value is  i'm i'm i'm happy to address that um  i don't believe that there is a high  cut-off for hemoglobin a1c  the current recommendations from  most clinical diabetes organizations  is that 